{"source_slots":{"s0":"Well, I hooked it up for twenty minutes and the lights stayed on—no sparks, no smell of melting insulation. So I’m calling it a win. Still need to label the switch box properly, though. Last week a student flipped the wrong lever and accidentally killed power to the projector during a documentary on Arctic wolves. Talk about bad timing. Now I’ve got half the class convinced the blackout was symbolic.","s2":"I picked up a label maker from the teacher next door—turns out she’s big on color-coding her science supplies, so she had an extra. Got the main circuits labeled in red and blue, nothing fancy. Only hiccup was realizing one of the breakers doesn’t actually go to anything I can find. Could be an old outlet buried behind drywall, or maybe it’s feeding the ghost light in the storage closet that flickers when it rains. Either way, I taped it off with a note. Not ideal, but it beats another wolf documentary blackout. Tool-wise, I’m set—just need to keep the kids from “experimenting” during free period.","s4":"Yeah, I left it taped off with a “Do Not Touch” sign and a doodle of a frowning octopus. Not OSHA-approved, probably, but it’s made the kids pause. I did sneak a multimeter in there after school—no load, no voltage, just dead silence. Might be an old feed from when this building was a print shop. I’ll ask the custodian tomorrow; he’s been here forty years and remembers when the woodshop had asbestos insulation. If anyone knows about buried outlets and rogue wires, it’s him. Until then, I’m calling it a controlled mystery.","s5":""It sounds like you've successfully navigated a complex, multi-layered problem, moving from a power failure to an investigation into the building's internal wiring. Since you have identified the potential source of the intermittent issue—a possible old feed or "rogue wires"—your next steps should be focused on **documentation and safety protocols** rather than just the fix itself.**

If the building was a "print shop," there may be significant structural or environmental risks. While your "Do Not Touch" note with the octopus is a humorous touch, your priority now must shift from *discovery* to *safety*.

**What is the specific action you need next?** Are you looking to understand the safety risk, or are you looking for advice on how to safely decommission the old, possibly non-standard wiring?""}}
